    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The True Math of the Offer\n\nAt first glance, Google\u2019s offer appears to be an annualized **$552,000/yr** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k equity avg). However, Google uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)** for initial RSU grants:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 |\n| **RSU Vesting** | $356,400 *(33%)* | $356,400 *(33%)* | $237,600 *(22%)* | $129,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Total Comp (Pre-Refresh)** | **$638,400** | **$638,400** | **$519,600** | **$411,600** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n* **Years 1 & 2:** You get an immediate cash flow bump from your current ~$526,000 at Meta to **~$638,400** (+21%).\n* **Years 3 & 4 (The Cliff):** Your initial equity drops steeply. While Google\u2019s annual refresher grants (which typically vest 25% per year) will help backfill this, they are tied to performance ratings and are not guaranteed.\n* **Base Salary is a Step Down:** Meta pays you $240,000 base; Google is offering $235,000 (though Google\u2019s 20% bonus target vs. Meta\u2019s 15% offsets this slightly, giving you $282k total cash vs. $276k at Meta).\n* **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Google provided **$0 sign-on**, despite poaching a happy, well-compensated Meta IC6 who is leaving behind unvested equity and performance cycle timing.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**It is a solid initial offer, but not a compelling one for someone who wasn't actively looking.** \n\n$1.08M in RSUs is right around the 50th\u201360th percentile for Google L6 PMs in the Bay Area. However, because you are walking away from an established reputation at Meta and a steady stream of Meta refreshes, accepting this as-is carries transition risk without sufficient upside.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Verdict: Negotiate\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away yet.** \n\nYou have maximum leverage:\n1. **Inbound Poach:** Google recruited you; you were not actively looking.\n2. **Strong Alternative (BATNA):** You are happy at Meta with strong existing compensation ($526k/yr).\n3. **Band Headroom:** Your base salary of $235k is well below the top of the posted range ($271k), and L6 equity grants can stretch up to $1.2M\u2013$1.35M for competitive hires.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nWhen negotiating with Google, the easiest levers for recruiters to move are **Sign-on Bonus** (one-time cash budget) and **Equity (GSUs)**. Base salary increases require strict band calibration, but you should still ask to at least beat your current Meta base.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Counter-Offer Target | Realistic Landing Target |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$80,000** | $40,000 \u2013 $60,000 |\n| **4-Year RSUs** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000** | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n\n#### Why this structure works:\n* **Base ($255k ask):** Protects you from taking a step backward on base salary and moves closer to the posted $271k upper limit.\n* **Sign-on ($80k ask):** Compensates for the unvested Meta RSUs you forfeit in the immediate short term and bridges any bonus cycle misalignment.\n* **Equity ($1.25M ask):** Lifts Year 1 & 2 compensation past ~$700k and cushions the Year 3/4 drop-off.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Email\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n> \n> *Thank you again for putting together this offer. I really enjoyed meeting [Hiring Manager / Team] and am genuinely excited about the scope of the [Product/Team] role.*\n> \n> *Since I wasn't actively looking and am very well-positioned as an IC6 at Meta\u2014where I currently earn $240k base ($276k total cash) with $250k+/yr in vesting equity and upcoming refresher cycles\u2014making a move needs to represent a clear step forward to offset the transition risk and unvested equity I would be leaving behind.*\n> \n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, I\u2019d be looking for:*\n> * * **Base Salary:** $255,000 (to reflect an increase over my current base and align with the upper end of the posted band)*\n> * * **Sign-on Bonus:** $80,000 (to bridge forfeited unvested stock and bonus timing)*\n> * * **Equity (GSUs):** $1,250,000 over 4 years*\n> \n> *If we can bridge the gap on these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and join the team. Let me know what you think is possible to bring back to the compensation committee.\"*\n\n### Next Steps:\nIf Google meets you halfway (e.g., ~$245k base, $50k sign-on, $1.15M\u2013$1.2M stock), you will be looking at **~$715k in Year 1 total compensation**, which makes the transition financially worthwhile. If they refuse to budge at all, you are in a great spot to comfortably stay at Meta.",
