    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Numbers on the Revised Offer\n\nBecause Google front-loads equity over a 4-year schedule (**33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**), your revised offer breaks down as follows:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **RSU Vesting** | $422,400 *(33%)* | $422,400 *(33%)* | $281,600 *(22%)* | $153,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Total Compensation** | **$710,400** | **$710,400** | **$569,600\\*** | **$441,600\\*** |\n\n*\\*Note: Years 3 and 4 do not yet factor in annual refresher grants earned during Years 1 and 2, which will stack on top.*\n\n---\n\n### 2. How Google Handled Your Counter\n\nWhile Google declined a cash sign-on bonus, they **transferred that entire $200,000 into your equity grant** (raising it from $1.08M to $1.28M):\n* By adding $200k in equity, your Year 1 and Year 2 vesting jumped by **+$66,000/year**.\n* An equity grant of **$1.28M for an L6 PM** is near the top of the Google compensation band (85th+ percentile).\n* They matched your Meta base salary at **$240,000**, with a higher bonus target (20% vs. Meta\u2019s 15%), giving you **$288,000 in target cash** vs. your current $276,000.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Comparing Google vs. Meta\n\n* **Year 1 Delta:** You move from ~$476k\u2013$526k at Meta to **$710,400 at Google** (an immediate **+$185k to +$234k pre-tax gain** in Year 1).\n* **Two-Year Horizon:** You will earn **~$1.42M at Google** over the next 24 months, compared to ~$1.05M at Meta\u2014a **~$370,000 net upside**.\n* **The \"Unvested Stock\" Question:** By staying at Meta, you would vest $200,000 in equity over the next 12 months. At Google, you will vest **$422,400** in Year 1 alone. Google has completely covered your forfeited stock through front-loaded equity.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommendation: Take the Offer\n\n**Yes, you should accept this offer.** \n\nThis offer clears the bar financially and strategically:\n\n1. **They Hit Your Ceiling:** $1.28M equity + $240k base is effectively top-of-band for an L6 PM hire without a competing written counteroffer from another company.\n2. **Massive Short-Term Cash Flow:** ~$710k/yr in Years 1 and 2 provides substantial financial upside with zero downside to your base cash flow.\n3. **The Year 3/4 Cliff is Manageable:** Even before refreshers, Year 3 ($569.6k) beats your current Meta baseline. Once 2 years of standard L6 performance refreshers stack on top, your Year 3 total comp will likely sit around **$620,000\u2013$650,000**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Only Two Reasons to Decline\n\nYou should only pass on this offer if:\n1. **You are on track for an IC7 promotion at Meta within the next 6\u201312 months.** (Switching to Google resets your promotion timeline).\n2. **You strongly prefer Meta\u2019s culture, pace, or specific product scope** over Google's environment and feel the ~$370k two-year premium isn't worth the change in team dynamics.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Suggested Acceptance Script\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n> \n> *Thank you for working with the compensation committee to get the base to $240,000 and the equity to $1,280,000. I appreciate the effort to bridge the gap on the unvested equity I am leaving behind.*\n> \n> *I\u2019m excited about the team and the scope of the role, and I\u2019m ready to formally accept. Please send over the revised written offer letter and next steps for onboarding.\"*",
